      "max_user_property_value_length": 36,
      "max_items_per_event": 200,
      "max_custom_item_params": 27,
      "max_payload_size_bytes": 8192,
      "timestamp_backdating_limit_hours": 72,
      "validation_behavior_options": ["RELAXED", "ENFORCE_RECOMMENDATIONS"],
      "http_method": "POST",
      "content_type": "application/json",
      "success_response": "2xx with no body content (typically 204 No Content)"
    }
  },
  "notes": "Google Analytics shared cloud destination. Category: Analytics. Segment has GA4 Cloud (Actions) destination (slug: actions-google-analytics-4) and GA4 Web (Actions) destination (slug: actions-google-analytics-4-web). Both use the GA4 Measurement Protocol (v2) endpoint at https://www.google-analytics.com/mp/collect. The Cloud Actions variant sends server-side via Measurement Protocol, while the Web Actions variant uses client-side gtag.js. For payload parity testing, both Segment and RudderStack produce identical GA4 Measurement Protocol JSON payloads, differing only in envelope metadata (library name, messageId format, presence of rudderId/originalTimestamp/sentAt/receivedAt fields). The GA4 Measurement Protocol requires client_id and at least one event with name and params. session_id and engagement_time_msec are strongly recommended for real-time reporting. Ecommerce events follow GA4 recommended event naming conventions (purchase, view_item, add_to_cart, begin_checkout, etc.) with structured items arrays. Custom dimensions map to event params; custom metrics map to numeric event params; user properties are set via the user_properties object. GA4 does not return error details for malformed payloads — it returns 2xx even for invalid data. Validation should use the debug endpoint at https://www.google-analytics.com/debug/mp/collect."
}
